window.docContents[18].resolve({"/#division-as-iterated-subtraction":{"contents":"Just as multiplication is iterated addition and exponentiation is iterated multiplication, division can be understood as iterated subtraction.\nThe very first description of recursive functions in this book presents a version of division that terminates when the divisor is not zero, but that Lean does not accept.\nProving that division terminates requires the use of a fact about inequalities.\n\nLean cannot prove that this definition of division terminates:\n\n\n\n\n\nThat's a good thing, because it doesn't!\nWhen  is , value of  does not decrease, so the program is an infinite loop.\n\nRewriting the function to take evidence that  is not  allows Lean to automatically prove termination:\n\nThis definition of  terminates because the first argument  is smaller on each recursive call.\nThis can be expressed using a termination_by clause:\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming, Proving, and Performance\u0009More Inequalities","header":"8.4.4. Division as Iterated Subtraction","id":"/#division-as-iterated-subtraction"},"/#hello-world-parts":{"contents":"When Lean is invoked with the --run option, it invokes the program's main definition.\nIn programs that do not take command-line arguments,  should have type .\nThis means that  is not a function, because there are no arrows (→) in its type.\nInstead of being a function that has side effects,  consists of a description of effects to be carried out.\n\nAs discussed in the preceding chapter,  is the simplest inductive type.\nIt has a single constructor called  that takes no arguments.\nLanguages in the C tradition have a notion of a void function that does not return any value at all.\nIn Lean, all functions take an argument and return a value, and the lack of interesting arguments or return values can be signaled by using the  type instead.\nIf  represents a single bit of information,  represents zero bits of information.\n\n is the type of a program that, when executed, will either throw an exception or return a value of type .\nDuring execution, this program may have side effects.\nThese programs are referred to as  actions.\nLean distinguishes between evaluation of expressions, which strictly adheres to the mathematical model of substitution of values for variables and reduction of sub-expressions without side effects, and execution of  actions, which rely on an external system to interact with the world.\n is a function from strings to  actions that, when executed, write the given string to standard output.\nBecause this action doesn't read any interesting information from the environment in the process of emitting the string,  has type .\nIf it did return something interesting, then that would be indicated by the  action having a type other than .\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Hello, World!\u0009Running a Program","header":"2.1.1. Anatomy of a Greeting","id":"/#hello-world-parts"}});